import{_ as t,o,c as i,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Symbiotic Feedback Loop Mechanism","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/0025-共生反馈循环机制.md","filePath":"en/0025-共生反馈循环机制.md"}'),n={name:"en/0025-共生反馈循环机制.md"};function s(r,e,l,c,d,h){return o(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="symbiotic-feedback-loop-mechanism" tabindex="-1">Symbiotic Feedback Loop Mechanism <a class="header-anchor" href="#symbiotic-feedback-loop-mechanism" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Symbiotic Feedback Loop Mechanism&quot;">​</a></h1><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document No.</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Module</td><td>Anchor Mechanism and Symbiosis Principles</td></tr><tr><td>Keywords</td><td>Feedback Loop, Positive Feedback, Negative Feedback, Closed-Loop Optimization, Self-Adaptation</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="i-basic-concept-and-significance-of-the-feedback-loop" tabindex="-1">I. Basic Concept and Significance of the Feedback Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-basic-concept-and-significance-of-the-feedback-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. Basic Concept and Significance of the Feedback Loop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The symbiotic feedback loop mechanism is the core operating mechanism through which the anchor symbiotic network realizes self-optimization and self-evolution. The so-called feedback loop refers to the process in which the output results of a system return as input to the system, thereby affecting its subsequent operation. In the symbiotic network, every cultivator&#39;s cultivation achievements, every anchor&#39;s operating data, and the effect of every system optimization are re-entered into the system as feedback information, driving the entire symbiotic network to evolve continuously.</p><p>The core significance of the feedback loop mechanism lies in: it transforms the symbiotic network from a static infrastructure into a dynamic &quot;living organism&quot; capable of self-learning and self-optimization. In the age of the predatory system, once a cultivation technique was created, it tended to become rigid; later practitioners could only patch within the framework of their predecessors, finding it hard to achieve fundamental breakthroughs. The symbiotic network, through the feedback loop mechanism, can continuously collect massive cultivation data, constantly optimize cultivation systems, and make the evolutionary path ever wider.</p><p>The feedback loops in the symbiotic network are divided into two basic types:</p><ol><li><strong>Positive feedback loop</strong>: the output of the system reinforces the input of the system, continuously enhancing the operating effect of the system. For example, cultivators&#39; breakthrough feedback optimizes the cultivation system; the optimized system helps more cultivators break through; more breakthroughs bring more optimization feedback</li><li><strong>Negative feedback loop</strong>: the output of the system suppresses the input of the system, keeping the system&#39;s operation within a stable range. For example, when energy consumption in a certain region is too fast, the system automatically reduces the energy allocation of that region, preventing energy depletion</li></ol><p>The positive feedback loop is responsible for &quot;growth and evolution&quot;, while the negative feedback loop is responsible for &quot;stability and balance&quot;; the two complement each other, jointly maintaining the healthy operation of the symbiotic network.</p><h2 id="ii-the-five-stage-closed-loop-of-the-feedback-loop" tabindex="-1">II. The Five-Stage Closed Loop of the Feedback Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-the-five-stage-closed-loop-of-the-feedback-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. The Five-Stage Closed Loop of the Feedback Loop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The feedback loop of the symbiotic network follows the five-stage closed loop of &quot;collection — transmission — processing — execution — verification&quot;, forming a complete self-optimization process:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Name</th><th>Core Task</th><th>Executing Subject</th><th>Output Product</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stage 1</td><td>Data Collection</td><td>Collect raw data such as cultivator status, anchor operation, system effects</td><td>Planet Access Anchor</td><td>Raw data stream</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 2</td><td>Data Transmission</td><td>Converge and transmit collected data upward along the anchor network</td><td>Anchor nodes at all levels</td><td>Aggregated data set</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 3</td><td>Data Processing</td><td>Analyze, model, and deduce the aggregated data, generating optimization plans</td><td>Plane Core Anchor / Cosmic Origin Anchor</td><td>Optimization instruction set</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 4</td><td>Plan Execution</td><td>Distribute optimization plans to nodes at all levels and implement them</td><td>Anchor management institutions at all levels</td><td>Execution results</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 5</td><td>Effect Verification</td><td>Collect new data after execution, verify optimization effects, and enter the next loop</td><td>Planet Access Anchor</td><td>Verification report</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_2-1-data-collection-stage" tabindex="-1">2.1 Data Collection Stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-1-data-collection-stage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.1 Data Collection Stage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Data collection is the starting point of the feedback loop, automatically completed by Planet Access Anchors. Collected content includes but is not limited to:</p><ul><li><strong>Individual cultivation data</strong>: energy fluctuations, realm changes, system fit, cultivation duration, breakthrough records, etc.</li><li><strong>Anchor operating data</strong>: energy throughput, law stability, network latency, fault records, load conditions, etc.</li><li><strong>System effect data</strong>: cultivation efficiency, bottleneck distribution, success rate, side effects of each cultivation system, etc.</li><li><strong>Environmental status data</strong>: regional energy concentration, law activity, ecological indicators, abnormal events, etc.</li></ul><p>Data collection follows the principles of &quot;minimum necessity&quot; and &quot;privacy protection&quot;, collecting only evolution-related data, not involving individuals&#39; privacy information. All collected data is anonymized before transmission.</p><h3 id="_2-2-data-transmission-stage" tabindex="-1">2.2 Data Transmission Stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-2-data-transmission-stage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.2 Data Transmission Stage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The collected raw data converges upward along the anchor network. During transmission, preliminary aggregation and deduplication are performed at each anchor level, reducing the data processing pressure of higher-level nodes.</p><p>Data transmission adopts the dual mode of &quot;batch + real-time&quot;: for general cultivation statistics, batch transmission mode is used (aggregated once per standard cycle); for time-sensitive data such as abnormal events and emergency alarms, real-time transmission mode is used, ensuring that higher-level nodes can respond at the first moment.</p><h3 id="_2-3-data-processing-stage" tabindex="-1">2.3 Data Processing Stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-3-data-processing-stage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.3 Data Processing Stage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Data processing is the core link of the feedback loop, completed by the Information Cores of Plane Core Anchors and the Cosmic Origin Anchor. The processing process includes:</p><ol><li><strong>Data cleaning</strong>: remove noise data, outliers, and duplicate records, ensuring data quality</li><li><strong>Pattern recognition</strong>: through machine learning and law deduction, identify hidden patterns and trends in the data</li><li><strong>Causal analysis</strong>: analyze the causal relationships between cultivation effects and various factors, identifying key influencing factors</li><li><strong>Plan generation</strong>: based on analysis results, generate specific optimization plans, including system adjustments, resource allocation optimization, anchor parameter calibration, etc.</li><li><strong>Risk assessment</strong>: conduct simulation deduction of optimization plans, assessing possible risks and side effects</li></ol><p>The results of data processing are output in the form of &quot;optimization instruction sets&quot;, containing specific adjustment parameters and execution steps.</p><h3 id="_2-4-plan-execution-stage" tabindex="-1">2.4 Plan Execution Stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-4-plan-execution-stage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.4 Plan Execution Stage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The optimization instruction set is distributed downward along the anchor network and implemented by anchor management institutions at all levels. The execution process includes:</p><ul><li><strong>System updates</strong>: update the technique parameters of the Three Hundred Cultivation Systems, pushing them to all cultivators</li><li><strong>Resource adjustments</strong>: adjust the energy allocation and resource distribution plans of each region</li><li><strong>Anchor calibration</strong>: calibrate the operating parameters of anchors, optimizing energy conduction paths</li><li><strong>Mentor guidance</strong>: distribute new guidance points to the mentor network, improving teaching quality</li></ul><p>Plan execution follows the principle of &quot;gray-scale release&quot; — first pilot on a small scale, verify the effect, then promote comprehensively, reducing the risk of optimization plans.</p><h3 id="_2-5-effect-verification-stage" tabindex="-1">2.5 Effect Verification Stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-5-effect-verification-stage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.5 Effect Verification Stage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>After plan execution, the system recollects data to verify the optimization effects. Verification indicators include:</p><ul><li><strong>Cultivation efficiency improvement rate</strong>: the improvement of average cultivation efficiency after optimization compared to before</li><li><strong>Bottleneck breakthrough rate</strong>: the breakthrough ratio of original common bottlenecks</li><li><strong>Side effect occurrence rate</strong>: the occurrence rate of new problems caused by the optimization plan</li><li><strong>Individual satisfaction</strong>: cultivators&#39; subjective evaluation of the optimization plan</li></ul><p>Verification results enter the next loop as new feedback data, forming continuous closed-loop optimization.</p><h2 id="iii-typical-feedback-loop-scenarios" tabindex="-1">III. Typical Feedback Loop Scenarios <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-typical-feedback-loop-scenarios" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. Typical Feedback Loop Scenarios&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_3-1-cultivation-system-optimization-loop" tabindex="-1">3.1 Cultivation System Optimization Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-cultivation-system-optimization-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1 Cultivation System Optimization Loop&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This is the most common feedback loop scenario. When a cultivator encounters a bottleneck while cultivating a certain system, the bottleneck data is collected and uploaded; higher-level nodes analyze and find that this bottleneck is common, generating a system optimization plan; the optimized system is pushed to cultivators; cultivators successfully break through the bottleneck using the new system; the breakthrough data is collected again, verifying the optimization effect.</p><h3 id="_3-2-energy-dispatch-optimization-loop" tabindex="-1">3.2 Energy Dispatch Optimization Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-2-energy-dispatch-optimization-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.2 Energy Dispatch Optimization Loop&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Energy consumption in a certain region suddenly surges, causing the local energy pool to be insufficient. After collecting this anomaly, the anchor node borrows energy from adjacent regions through horizontal connections; at the same time, the anomaly data is reported to higher-level nodes; the higher-level nodes analyze the cause of the anomaly (such as a large-scale breakthrough in progress in that region), generating a long-term energy dispatch optimization plan; after plan execution, the energy supply of that region returns to stability.</p><h3 id="_3-3-anchor-maintenance-optimization-loop" tabindex="-1">3.3 Anchor Maintenance Optimization Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-anchor-maintenance-optimization-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3 Anchor Maintenance Optimization Loop&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Law Core of a certain anchor experiences slight drift; the Calibration Ring automatically performs preliminary calibration; at the same time, the drift data is reported upward; higher-level nodes analyze and find that the drift is related to geological activity, generating an anchor position fine-tuning plan; after plan execution, the stability of the anchor significantly improves; new operating data verifies the maintenance effect.</p><h2 id="iv-regulation-and-boundaries-of-the-feedback-loop" tabindex="-1">IV. Regulation and Boundaries of the Feedback Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-regulation-and-boundaries-of-the-feedback-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. Regulation and Boundaries of the Feedback Loop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Although the feedback loop is powerful, it is not unconstrained. The symbiotic civilization has established clear regulation mechanisms and boundary rules:</p><ol><li><strong>Manual review mechanism</strong>: major optimization plans (involving law parameter adjustments, cross-plane energy dispatch, etc.) must undergo manual review and cannot rely entirely on automated processing</li><li><strong>Rollback mechanism</strong>: every optimization plan is equipped with a rollback plan; if serious problems occur after execution, it can quickly restore to the pre-optimization state</li><li><strong>Ethical boundaries</strong>: the optimization goals of the feedback loop must comply with symbiotic ethics; individual rights must not be sacrificed to improve overall efficiency</li><li><strong>Diversity protection</strong>: the feedback loop must not excessively pursue the &quot;optimal solution&quot; and eliminate the diversity of cultivation methods; the system retains a certain proportion of &quot;non-mainstream&quot; cultivation paths</li></ol><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The symbiotic feedback loop mechanism is the &quot;soul&quot; of the anchor symbiotic network, giving the entire network the ability to self-learn, self-optimize, and self-evolve. Through the continuous operation of the five-stage closed loop, the symbiotic network can constantly improve cultivation efficiency, optimize resource allocation, and enhance system stability, ultimately driving all symbiotic lives to realize the common progress of the Myriad Races. Understanding the feedback loop mechanism is the key to understanding why the symbiotic civilization can continuously evolve.</p><h2 id="related-documents" tabindex="-1">Related Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-documents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related Documents&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>21-共生网络运作原理 (Symbiotic Network Operating Principles)</li><li>27-锚点校准与维护 (Anchor Calibration and Maintenance)</li><li>29-共生度评估体系 (Symbiosis Index Evaluation System)</li></ul>',44)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
